        {
          "say": "Explain how annual leave accrual works for employees.",
          "reply": "Here's how annual leave accrual generally works, based on our policy: **The basic idea**\nAnnual leave is earned (accrued) over time rather than granted all at once. As you complete service, you build up a leave entitlement that you can then take, subject to approval. **How accrual typically runs**\n- **Accrual rate:** Leave accrues at a set rate per period of service — commonly expressed as a number of days earned per month worked. Over a full year of service this adds up to your annual entitlement.\n- **Accrual basis:** Accrual is usually tied to actual service time, so it builds up as you work and pauses during unpaid absences.\n- **Balance components:** Your leave record generally shows an **entitled** amount, an **accrued/used** amount, and any **pending** requests. What you can actually take is the **remaining** balance.\n- **Carry-over:** Many policies allow unused leave to carry into the next year, sometimes with a cap or an expiry date. Any excess beyond the cap may lapse.\n- **Timing of use:** Leave is normally requested and approved in advance, and accrual may need to reach a minimum before leave can be taken. **Important**\nThe exact accrual rate, carry-over cap, and any waiting period are set by GOFSCO's leave policy, and I don't have those specific figures in front of me here. I've kept this to the general mechanics rather than quoting numbers I can't verify. If you'd like your own figures — how much you've accrued, used, and have remaining — I can look at your personal leave balance. Just say the word. For anything that changes your leave record (submitting or adjusting a request), you'd do that through **Agent** or **My**.",
