        {
          "say": "What is the difference between gross pay and net pay?",
          "reply": "**Gross pay** is the total amount earned before any deductions. It's what your salary structure adds up to for the period — basic salary plus any allowances, overtime, or other earnings. **Net pay** is what actually reaches you after deductions are taken out. It's gross pay minus things like: - Employee social insurance contributions\n- Income tax\n- Any other authorised deductions (e.g. loan installments, if applicable) So the relationship is: **Net pay = Gross pay − Total deductions** A few practical notes: - **Gross** is the figure used for things like salary certificates and end-of-service calculations.\n- **Net** is the figure that shows on your bank transfer and is usually the headline number on a payslip.\n- If you want to see how this breaks down for you personally, your payslip in Nibras shows earnings and deductions line by line, with the net at the bottom. If you'd like, I can walk you through the typical components of a payslip so you know which lines sit above the gross total and which sit below it.",
